th a stylus or fingernail; the touch mechanism worked fine regardless of surface scuffs. Modern capacitive touchscreens rely on an even electric field near the surface—deep scratches can disrupt that field and cause dead spots, plus they feel unpleasant to the finger. --- ### The modern smartphone changed the rules When smartphones moved to large, capacitive, edge-to-edge glass displays, the equation flipped: - **Display clarity and brightness** became paramount. Even fine scratches scatter light, reducing contrast and making the screen look permanently dirty. - **Finger touch and glide** matter immensely. A scratched, soft plastic surface feels draggy and cheap, and it’s harder to apply an oleophobic coating that lasts. - **Structural support for the display.** A modern OLED panel is thinner than paper. If you put a flexible plastic cover on it, pressing hard with your thumb would deform the cover into the display, potentially damaging pixels. Glass acts as a stiff shield that distributes pressure. - **Consumer perception.** Through years of market research and sales data, it turned out that people overwhelmingly prefer a screen that stays pristine and glossy—even if it comes with the risk of shattering—to one that looks beat up after a few weeks. Right or wrong, shiny glass sells; scratched plastic feels like a downgrade. That’s why the industry stuck with glass and then poured billions into making it more impact-resistant (Gorilla Glass, Ceramic Shield, etc.), even though it’ll never be as shatterproof as polycarbonate. --- ### The compromise we actually live with Interestingly, many people solve this exact dilemma by putting a **plastic screen protector** on their glass phone. That combo gets you: - A hard, rigid base layer (glass) for clarity and structural stiffness. - A sacrificial, soft top layer (the plastic film) that absorbs scratches and some impact energy, preventing the glass beneath from cracking. In a sense, that’s just the best of both worlds—and if your glass phone always cracks, a good quality hybrid screen protector (or even a thick liquid glass one) might help replicate the durability of your old flip phone while keeping the premium display quality. No one’s saying your frustration is wrong. If someone made a modern, large, high-resolution plastic screen that didn’t scratch, didn’t degrade optically, and felt solid under the finger, they’d clean up. That material just doesn’t exist yet at consumer scale—but that’s exactly what the foldable phone industry is chasing with ultra-thin glass and polymer hybrids. In the meantime, we’re all choosing shatter-prone glass because the alternative—a screen that quickly looks like it’s been through a sandstorm—turned out to be a dealbreaker for most people.
